ntromere protein C gene leads to mitotic disarray and early embryo death</article-title><source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA</source><volume>95</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>1136</fpage><lpage>1141</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9448298</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Leeetal2000"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>K.K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gruenbaum</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Spann</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Wilson</surname><given-names>K.L.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title><italic>C. elegans</italic> nuclear envelope proteins emerin, MAN1, laminin, and nucleoporins reveal unique timing of nuclear envelope breakdown during mitosis</article-title><source>Mol. Biol. Cell</source><volume>11</volume><year>2000</year><fpage>3089</fpage><lpage>3099</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10982402</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Maneyetal2000"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Maney</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ginkel</surname><given-names>L.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hunter</surname><given-names>A.W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Wordeman</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The kinetochore of higher eukaryotesa molecular view</article-title><source>Int. Rev. Cytol</source><volume>194</volume><year>2000</year><fpage>67</fpage><lpage>131</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10494625</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="MeluhandKoshland1995"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Meluh</surname><given-names>P.B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Koshland</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Evidence that the MIF2 gene of <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic> encodes a centromere protein with homology to the mammalian centromere protein CENP-C</article-title><source>Mol. Biol. Cell</source><volume>6</volume><year>1995</year><fpage>793</fpage><lpage>807</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7579695</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Meluhetal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Meluh</surname><given-names>P.B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Glowczewski</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Koshland</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>M.M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cse4p is a component of the core centromere of <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic></article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>94</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>607</fpage><lpage>613</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9741625</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Mooreetal1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>L.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Morrison</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Roth</surname><given-names>M.B.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>HCP-1, a protein involved in chromosome segregation, is localized to the centromere of mitotic chromosomes in <italic>Caenorhabditis elegans</italic></article-title><source>J. Cell Biol</source><volume>147</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>471</fpage><lpage>480</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10545493</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Nicklas1971"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nicklas</surname><given-names>R.B.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mitosis</article-title><source>Adv. Cell Biol</source><volume>2</volume><year>1971</year><fpage>225<